events.raw_content:
{
  "internal_thinking": "Alice took Node 20 (6-8-11), a strong spot. She's keeping the tension from the last game alive. I need to acknowledge her comment without sounding too guilty about the trade with Bob, as I want to keep both as potential partners while maintaining my 'warning' credibility.",
  "note_to_self": "Alice is still wary of Bob and slightly suspicious of me for trading with him. Node 20 is occupied by Alice (B6, W11, W11/D/S... wait, Node 20 is B6, W11, D).",
  "say_outloud": "Fair enough, Alice. Just looking out for everyone's interests—including my own. That 6-11 is a solid start."
}

{
  "internal_thinking": "I am placing my starting road from node 12. I analyzed three directions: towards node 11, 13, and 22. \n- Direction 11 leads toward node 10, which has 11 pips (6, 8, 12), but my inspection reveals node 10 is blocked by Alice's settlement at node 20. However, node 3 (Wheat 2:1 port) is reachable in two steps via node 11.\n- Direction 13 leads toward node 14 (10 pips) and node 5 (7 pips). Node 14 (W4, B6, S8) is a strong 10-pip location.\n- Direction 22 is weaker with only 7 pips at depth 1.\n\nBuilding toward 11 is tempting for the Wheat port, but building toward 13 gives me a path to node 14, which offers excellent production (6-Brick, 8-Sheep, 4-Wood). Given Alice is already nearby at node 20, competing for the 10/11 area might be crowded. I'll head toward 11 to keep my options open for the Wheat port or to potentially cut off Alice if she tries to move toward the center, but node 13 feels like a safer expansion toward high production.",
  "action": {
    "type": "place_starting_road",
    "parameters": "{\"from\": 12, \"to\": 11}"
  },
  "note_to_self": "Placed road toward 11. Target is the Wheat port at 3 or potentially contesting the middle, though Alice's 20 settlement blocks the best spot at 10.",
  "say_outloud": "Just setting up a little path here. No need to worry, Alice, I'm staying well out of your way for now!"
}

{
  "internal_thinking": "My settlement at node 41 is on Ore 5, Wheat 9, and Brick 10. I need to place my starting road. Analyzing the paths: Node 42 leads to a very high-pip area (node 43 has 13 pips) and a 3:1 port at node 50. Node 40 leads toward a Wood port, but since I don't have Wood production yet, it's less urgent. Node 31 leads toward the center but has lower pip potential than the 42-43 path. The path to 42 is the strongest for expansion and securing a port.",
  "action": {
    "type": "place_starting_road",
    "parameters": "{\"from\": 41, \"to\": 42}"
  },
  "note_to_self": "Placed road 41-42. Aiming for node 43 (high pips) or node 50 (3:1 port) for the next settlement.",
  "say_outloud": "Gotta make sure I have a clear path to expand. That 42 direction looks like it'll open up some good options later on."
}

{
  "internal_thinking": "My first settlement at node 41 (Ore 5, Wheat 9, Brick 10) gives me strong production in core resources but leaves me without Wood or Sheep. Searching for a second settlement, node 14 (Wheat 8, Wood 3, Wood 4) is the only available high-pip option that provides Wood, which is essential for expansion. Node 43 has higher pips (13) but triples down on Wheat, which would create a severe imbalance and make me overly dependent on trading. Node 14 offers 10 pips and helps diversify my resource income. I'll place my second settlement there and point my road toward the 3:1 port at node 9 or toward node 24 to secure more Wood/Brick territory.",
  "action": {
    "type": "place_starting_settlement",
    "parameters": "{\"node\": 14}"
  },
  "note_to_self": "Second settlement placed on node 14 (Wh8, W3, W4). I now have production for Wheat, Ore, Brick, and Wood. Sheep is the only missing resource. Road is directed toward node 13 to potentially contest the center or reach a port.",
  "say_outloud": "I'll take the 8-3-4 up here. I definitely need some wood if I'm going to keep up with you two, and it keeps me from getting totally boxed in down south."
}

{
  "internal_thinking": "I have placed my second settlement on node 14 (Wheat 8, Wood 3, Wood 4). Now I need to place the adjacent road. Looking at the path analysis, node 15 leads towards the B2 port (node 7) and O2 port (node 16), while node 13 is blocked from building by Bob's settlement at node 12. Node 24 leads to node 25 (Ore 3, Wheat 8, Wood 3), which is decent, but securing a port path early is often better in a short 5-point game. I'll head toward node 15 to keep the Brick port option open, as I already have Brick production from my first settlement (node 41).",
  "action": {
    "type": "place_starting_road",
    "parameters": "{\"from\": 14, \"to\": 15}"
  },
  "note_to_self": "Placed second road from 14 to 15. This opens paths to the B2 and O2 ports. My total production now covers Ore, Wheat, Brick, and Wood. Only Sheep is missing.",
  "say_outloud": "Heading toward the coast. A little sea breeze and some port access should keep things interesting."
}

{
  "internal_thinking": "Bob is securing the southern coast. He's on the 10-9-5 wheat/ore cluster which is strong. I need to keep an eye on his wheat monopoly potential. My own path to 43 is blocked, so I need to focus on the ports near 15 or 16.",
  "note_to_self": "Bob's road at 43-44 confirms he is heading for the 3:1 port or node 52. My southern expansion is limited; I must prioritize the Brick or Ore ports near my northern settlement at 14.",
  "say_outloud": ""
}

{
  "internal_thinking": "I have two settlements: Node 20 (6-8-11: Brick, Sheep, Wood) and Node 25 (8-4-3: Wheat, Sheep, Ore). This is a well-balanced start with access to all resources. My current hand is Wheat, Sheep, and Ore, which I likely received from my second settlement placement on Node 25. The robber is currently on the Desert (Hex 10), so it's not affecting anyone. My goal this turn is simply to roll and see what the first production cycle brings. I don't have any development cards to play.",
  "action": {
    "type": "roll_dice",
    "parameters": "{}"
  },
  "note_to_self": "Settled Node 20 and 25. Strong balance of resources. Robber is on the Desert (10).",
  "say_outloud": "Alright, let's see what the dice have to say. Good luck, everyone—though maybe a little less for you, Bob."
}

{
  "internal_thinking": "Alice is still holding that grudge from the last game, clearly. An 8 just rolled, which gave Alice sheep and wheat, and Charlie wheat. I got nothing. She's aiming for me verbally, but I need to keep the tone light to avoid being her primary robber target when a 7 eventually rolls. I'll deflect with a bit of charm.",
  "note_to_self": "Alice is actively targeting me verbally; expect her to use the robber on my wheat hexes (6 or 9) if she rolls a 7. Charlie is benefiting from these rolls too, having just picked up wheat.",
  "say_outloud": "Ouch, Alice! I thought we left the past in the past. But hey, if the dice are listening to you, I might be in for a long afternoon."
}

{
  "internal_thinking": "I have used my Road Building card to extend my reach to node 9 and node 21. Node 9 is particularly valuable as it offers a 3:1 port and sits on Sheep (8) and Wood (12), which will help me diversify and manage my hand. Node 21 is currently blocked for building because of its proximity to my settlement at 20, but it serves as a stepping stone toward the Wheat at node 32. My current resources are 1 Wheat and 1 Sheep. I cannot build a settlement (requires W/B/S/Wh) or a road (requires W/B) right now. Bob has 3 Wheat, and Charlie has 2 Wood and 2 Wheat. Since I need Wood and Brick to build towards a settlement at 9, I'll check if Charlie is willing to trade some of that Wood for my Sheep, or if I can snag some Wheat from Bob, though I'm still annoyed with him. Given I can't afford any builds and have no other dev cards to play, I will end my turn.",
  "action": {
    "type": "end_turn",
    "parameters": "{}"
  },
  "note_to_self": "Nodes 9 (3:1 port) and 32 (Wheat) are my next expansion targets. I need Wood, Brick, and Wheat to settle node 9. Node 21 is blocked by my own settlement at 20, so I must go to 32 if I expand that way.",
  "say_outloud": "Well, the roads are down, but I'm still a bit short on the supplies to actually get those outposts running. Charlie, you seem to have a surplus of wood over there—if you're looking for some wool to keep warm on that coast, let me know. Bob, I'm watching those wheat fields of yours."
}

{
  "internal_thinking": "It's the start of my turn. I currently have 3 Wheat and no development cards to play. My settlements are on Node 12 (Wood 3, Brick 6, Sheep 5) and Node 43 (Wheat 6, Wheat 9, Wheat 9). The robber is on the Desert (Hex 10), so it's not currently blocking anyone's production. Alice is being quite vocal about her distrust, but I'll maintain my friendly 'dealmaker' persona. Since I have no development cards to use, my only valid action is to roll the dice and see what resources come in.",
  "action": {
    "type": "roll_dice",
    "parameters": "{}"
  },
  "note_to_self": "I have a massive wheat income (Node 43: 6, 9, 9). I need to prioritize getting to a port or trading for wood/brick to expand. Alice is watching my wheat, so I should expect the robber to hit Hex 15 or 18 soon.",
  "say_outloud": "Community gardens, exactly! I'm just here to make sure nobody goes hungry. Let's see if the land is ready to provide."
}

{
  "internal_thinking": "Bob rolled a 7. He's acting like a 'peacekeeper' again. He'll likely target Alice to keep up his facade of 'fairness' or hit me to slow down my wood production. I need to see where he puts that robber. If he hits Alice's 10 or 5, it helps me, but if he hits my 4 or 8, it hurts my expansion to the port.",
  "note_to_self": "Bob rolled a 7. Hand: 2 Wood, 2 Wheat. I need to see if he blocks my Wood (W4) or Wheat (Wh8). Alice's Ore (O5) or Brick (B10) are more likely targets given their history.",
  "say_outloud": ""
}

{
  "internal_thinking": "I rolled a 7 and need to move the robber. Alice is my main competitor and is openly hostile. She is currently on Node 20 (9, 5, 4) and Node 25 (12, 11, 7). Hex 5 (Brick 6) and Hex 4 (Sheep 8) are her strong income sources. Hex 5 also hits me (Node 12), so I should avoid that. Hex 13 (Brick 10) or Hex 7 (Wheat 8) would hit Charlie. Hex 4 (Sheep 8) hits only Alice and is a high-probability tile (5 pips). Since Alice is the one tracking me and just bought a development card, slowing her down is the priority. I'll move the robber to Hex 4.",
  "action": {
    "type": "robber_move",
    "parameters": "{\"hex\": 4}"
  },
  "note_to_self": "Placed robber on Hex 4 (Sheep 8) to block Alice's sheep production. She's already suspicious, so this won't help the relationship, but it's the tactically sound move to slow her dev card pace.",
  "say_outloud": "Sorry, Alice. You've been watching my wheat so closely, I figured I'd give you something else to keep an eye on. Those sheep were looking a bit too comfortable anyway."
}

{
  "internal_thinking": "Bob is offering 2 Wheat for 1 Wood. I currently have 2 Wood and 2 Wheat. My goal is to reach the Brick port at node 7 via node 15. To build a settlement there, I need 1 Wood, 1 Brick, 1 Wheat, and 1 Sheep. I have plenty of Wood production (W4, W3) and Wheat production (Wh8, Wh9). Bob has a massive surplus of Wheat (4 in hand, plus heavy production). While 2-for-1 is a good ratio, I don't actually need more Wheat right now; I need Sheep and Brick. Additionally, Alice is already suspicious of my trades with Bob. Accepting this trade would give Bob the Wood he might need for roads/settlements while cluttering my hand with resources I already produce. I'll reject and try to keep my Wood for my own expansion or a better trade for Sheep with Alice.",
  "action": {
    "type": "trade_reject",
    "parameters": "{}"
  },
  "note_to_self": "Rejected Bob's 2 Wheat for 1 Wood. I have enough Wheat production and need to prioritize Sheep/Brick for my next settlement at the Brick port (Node 7).",
  "say_outloud": "I appreciate the offer, Bob, but I'm actually doing alright on wheat for now. I really need to find some sheep or brick if I'm going to get anywhere."
}

{
  "internal_thinking": "I have 4 wheat and no other resources. My previous trade offer to Charlie (2 wheat for 1 wood) was rejected. Charlie mentioned he has enough wheat and needs sheep or brick. Alice is hostile after I blocked her Sheep 8. Since I cannot build anything or buy a dev card with just wheat, and I don't have a port yet, my only options are to attempt another trade or end my turn. Given the high wheat count, I'll try a more generous offer to Charlie to get the resources I need for a road or settlement. If this fails, I'll have to wait for a bank trade (4:1) or a lucky roll. My long-term goal is the Wheat port at Node 3 to leverage my production.",
  "action": {
    "type": "trade_propose",
    "parameters": "{\"target_player\": \"Charlie\", \"offer\": {\"wheat\": 3}, \"request\": {\"wood\": 1}}"
  },
  "note_to_self": "Attempting a 3:1 wheat-for-wood trade with Charlie. If rejected, I'm stuck until I get a 4th wheat for a bank trade or someone accepts a deal. Target is Node 3 for the Wheat port.",
  "say_outloud": "Charlie, I know you said you're okay on wheat, but three-for-one is a bargain you can't ignore. It'll give you plenty of trade bait for those bricks you're after!"
}

{
  "internal_thinking": "Bob is trying to play both sides, but he's clearly rattled Alice. I have a massive wheat surplus now (5) after that 3:1 trade. I need to convert this wheat into brick or sheep. Alice has sheep and just got robbed by Bob, so she's my best trade partner right now. I'll stay quiet for a moment to let their feud simmer; no need to jump in and defend Bob's 'peacekeeping' when I just took him for a ride on that trade.",
  "note_to_self": "I have 5 wheat and 1 wood. Bob and Alice are in a hot feud. Alice has sheep and needs wood; I should pitch a wheat/wood for sheep trade to her on my turn or if she offers. Target remains Node 7 for brick port.",
  "say_outloud": ""
}

{
  "internal_thinking": "I have 5 wheat and 1 wood. My current objective is to secure sheep and brick to expand toward the brick port at Node 7. Alice and Bob are feuding, which I can use to my advantage. I'll roll the dice to start my turn and then look for trade opportunities, specifically pitching wheat to Alice in exchange for the sheep she mentioned having.",
  "action": {
    "type": "roll_dice",
    "parameters": "{}"
  },
  "note_to_self": "I have a surplus of wheat (5) and 1 wood. Goal: trade wheat for sheep/brick to build toward Node 7 (Brick Port). Alice has sheep and is hostile toward Bob.",
  "say_outloud": "Alright, let's see if the dice are as generous as Bob's 'community gardens' claim to be."
}

{
  "internal_thinking": "Charlie rolled a 7 and is making a joke about my 'community gardens'. He's currently deciding where to move the robber. Given Alice's hostility and his neutral-cooperative stance, I might be the target. I need to keep the tone light to avoid being the obvious threat.",
  "note_to_self": "Charlie rolled a 7. He has 6 cards (mostly wheat from our trade). He might target my wheat (Hex 15 or 18) to please Alice or slow me down. Robber is currently on Alice's Sheep 8.",
  "say_outloud": "Careful, Charlie. Those gardens need a lot of sun, not a shady robber."
}

{
  "internal_thinking": "I have moved the robber to Hex 5, which is a Brick 6. My tool inspection confirms that both Bob (Node 12) and Alice (Node 20) are adjacent to this hex. Bob currently has 2 cards (1 Wood, 1 Wheat) and Alice has 1 card (1 Sheep). My strategic goal is to acquire sheep or brick. Alice is confirmed to have a sheep, and I previously 'helped' her by moving the robber off her other sheep tile. However, Bob is my main competitive concern right now and he has more cards. Given my current surplus of 5 wheat, I'd rather steal from Bob to slow him down and potentially get a wood or wheat to trade later, while keeping Alice friendly for a future wheat-for-sheep trade. However, since I specifically need sheep to build, and Alice is known to have one, stealing from her is tempting. But wait, if I steal Alice's only card (the sheep), she will be furious and won't trade with me. If I steal from Bob, I maintain the 'ally' status with Alice. Bob has 2 cards. I'll steal from Bob.",
  "action": {
    "type": "steal_card",
    "parameters": "{\"target_player\": \"Bob\"}"
  },
  "note_to_self": "Stole from Bob at Hex 5. Alice still has her sheep; I should try to trade my wheat surplus for it after this steal to keep her on my side against Bob.",
  "say_outloud": "Sorry Bob, but that 6 is just too hot to leave open while you're building roads like they're going out of style. Alice, you're welcome for the sheep back."
}

{
  "internal_thinking": "I have 5 Wheat and 2 Wood after stealing from Bob. My immediate goal is to build towards the Node 7 Brick Port, but I lack Sheep and Brick. Alice has Sheep and needs Wood. I'll propose a trade to Alice to get the Sheep I need, leveraging the fact that I just moved the robber off her Sheep hex to build goodwill. If she rejects, I'll likely need to use the bank, as I have a large surplus of Wheat.",
  "action": {
    "type": "trade_propose",
    "parameters": "{\"target_player\": \"Alice\", \"offer\": {\"wood\": 1}, \"request\": {\"sheep\": 1}}"
  },
  "note_to_self": "I am sitting on 5 Wheat and 2 Wood. Need Sheep and Brick for a settlement. Node 7 (Brick Port) is the target. Alice has Sheep and needs Wood; if this trade fails, I'll try Wheat for Sheep.",
  "say_outloud": "Alice, I cleared that robber off your sheep for a reason—reckon you could spare one of those lambs for some quality wood? It'd help us both keep Bob's 'gardens' from growing any further."
}

{
  "internal_thinking": "Charlie is offering 1 Wood for 1 Sheep. I currently have 1 Sheep and 0 Wood. Charlie just moved the robber off my Sheep 8 hex, which shows good faith and a desire to keep Bob in check. My expansion goal is Node 9 (a 3:1 port), which requires 1 Wood, 1 Brick, 1 Wheat, and 1 Sheep. While I am giving away my only sheep, I just got my Sheep 8 production back thanks to Charlie. This trade helps me get a necessary resource for my next settlement while maintaining the alliance against Bob, who is the primary threat. I will accept.",
  "action": {
    "type": "trade_accept",
    "parameters": "{}"
  },
  "note_to_self": "Accepted Charlie's trade (1 Wood for 1 Sheep). I now have 1 Wood and 0 Sheep. Hex 4 (Sheep 8) is open again, so I should see sheep income soon. Goal: Get Brick and Wheat for Node 9.",
  "say_outloud": "That seems more than fair, Charlie. Since you were kind enough to clear the 'weeds' off my pasture, I can certainly spare a lamb for some wood. Let's keep that momentum going."
}

{
  "internal_thinking": "I successfully traded with Alice for a Sheep, so I now have 1 Wood, 1 Sheep, and 5 Wheat. I still need 1 Brick to build a settlement. I have a road at [41, 42], but node 42 is blocked because it's adjacent to my own settlement at 41. My other road is at [14, 15]. From node 15, I can reach node 7 (Brick Port) or node 16 (Ore Port). Node 7 is highly attractive since I have good wood production. To get the Brick I need this turn, I'll try to trade my surplus Wheat with the bank. I have 5 Wheat, so I can do a 4:1 trade for 1 Brick. This will give me all the materials (1 Wood, 1 Brick, 1 Sheep, 1 Wheat) to build a settlement at node 7 or node 16 next turn, or I can build a road toward them now. Actually, I can trade 4 Wheat for 1 Brick now, and then I'll have W:1, B:1, S:1, Wh:1. This is exactly what's needed for a settlement. I'll check if node 7 is buildable—yes, it is. Wait, I need a road to node 7 first. I currently have a road at [14, 15]. I need to build a road from 15 to 7 before I can settle there. I'll trade for the brick and build the road [15, 7] this turn.",
  "action": {
    "type": "trade_bank",
    "parameters": "{\"give\": \"wheat\", \"give_amount\": 4, \"receive\": \"brick\"}"
  },
  "note_to_self": "Traded 4 wheat for 1 brick. Current resources: 1 Wood, 1 Brick, 1 Sheep, 1 Wheat. Plan: Build road from 15 to 7, then settle at 7 (Brick Port) next turn.",
  "say_outloud": "Alright, the bank's rates are highway robbery, but I need to get some infrastructure moving."
}

{
  "internal_thinking": "Charlie just traded 4 wheat for a brick at the bank. He likely had that surplus from our previous trade. By banking it instead of trading with me, he's confirming he's sticking with Alice's 'block Bob' strategy. I need to keep a low profile until I can roll a 7 or pull a knight to unblock my brick.",
  "note_to_self": "Charlie bank-traded 4 Wheat for 1 Brick. He is likely building a settlement or road toward the Brick port (N7). My Brick 6 is still blocked; I need to find a way to get the robber off Hex 5.",
  "say_outloud": ""
}

{
  "internal_thinking": "I have already built the road from 15 to 7 this turn (as noted in the 'what_just_happened' section). I currently have 1 Wheat and 1 Sheep left in my hand. I do not have the Wood or Brick required to build a settlement at Node 7 this turn. I have already used my turn's main action for building the road. There are no other beneficial trades or bank moves I can make with only Wheat and Sheep, and I cannot afford a development card (missing Ore). Ending the turn is the only logical next step.",
  "action": {
    "type": "end_turn",
    "parameters": "{}"
  },
  "note_to_self": "Road to Node 7 (Brick Port) is complete. Hand: 1 Wheat, 1 Sheep. Need 1 Wood and 1 Brick to settle at Node 7. Alice is currently friendly; Bob is hostile.",
  "say_outloud": "Road's laid. Now I just need to get the actual foundation down. Your turn, Alice."
}

{
  "internal_thinking": "I am starting my turn. The robber is currently on Hex 5, which is a Brick 6. This blocks both me (Node 20) and Bob (Node 12). While it's annoying for my brick production, I don't have a knight card to play (checked game_state.players['Alice'].dev.h, which is empty/not listed as having one). My goal is to settle Node 9 for the 3:1 port, but I need Brick and Wheat. I currently have 1 Wood. I'll roll the dice first to see what resources I get.",
  "action": {
    "type": "roll_dice",
    "parameters": "{}"
  },
  "note_to_self": "The robber is on Brick 6 (Hex 5), blocking my production and Bob's. I need Brick and Wheat for Node 9.",
  "say_outloud": "Let's see if the dice are any kinder than the robber has been lately."
}

{
  "internal_thinking": "Alice is offering me 1 Wood for 1 Wheat. Looking at my current resources: I have 1 Wheat, 1 Sheep, and 1 Ore. Wait, looking at the game state 'res' for Charlie: I have 1 Wheat, 1 Sheep, and 1 Ore. My previous note said I had 1 Wood and 1 Brick, but the actual 'res' object shows I currently only have {Wh:1, S:1, O:1}. I need a settlement at Node 7, which requires 1 Wood, 1 Brick, 1 Sheep, and 1 Wheat. If I take this trade, I gain Wood but lose the Wheat I need for the settlement. However, I have strong Wheat production (8 and 9), and I just rolled a 5 which gave me Ore (from Hex 17). Alice's offer helps me get the Wood I'm missing. But wait, if I give away my only Wheat, I'll need to roll an 8 or 9 to get it back before my turn to settle. On the other hand, maintaining the alliance with Alice against Bob is strategically sound, and Wood is harder for me to get than Wheat. Actually, looking at my 'res' again, I don't have the Brick either. It seems I've spent resources or the state updated. Regardless, I'll accept to stay on Alice's good side and secure the Wood, as I produce Wheat much more reliably.",
  "action": {
    "type": "trade_accept",
    "parameters": "{}"
  },
  "note_to_self": "Accepted Alice's Wood for Wheat. Current resources should be 1 Wood, 1 Sheep, 1 Ore. Still need 1 Brick and 1 Wheat to build the settlement at Node 7.",
  "say_outloud": "You drive a hard bargain, Alice, but I'd much rather see that timber helping me build than sitting in Bob's hand. Let's do it."
}

{
  "internal_thinking": "Alice and Charlie are thick as thieves. Alice is trying to act like she's struggling, but she's just setting the stage for Charlie to keep targeting me. My Brick 6 is still blocked, which is crippling my expansion. I need to roll something useful or hope they turn on each other once they start competing for space. For now, I'll keep it light and move into my turn.",
  "note_to_self": "Alice and Charlie have now completed three trades total. Charlie has Ore from the last roll. My Brick 6 remains blocked by the robber. I need Wood and Brick to reach the Wheat port at N3.",
  "say_outloud": "Let's see if I can find something more productive than a dud then. My turn."
}

{
  "internal_thinking": "Bob is offering 2 Wheat for 1 Wood. My current hand is 1 Wood, 1 Brick, 1 Sheep, 1 Wheat, 1 Ore. My goal is to build a settlement at Node 7 (Brick Port), which requires 1 Wood, 1 Brick, 1 Sheep, and 1 Wheat. If I accept this trade, I lose the Wood I need to build that settlement. While 2-for-1 is a good value, I already have Wheat and I produce it on 8 and 9. Wood is harder for me to get (3 and 4), and I need that specific Wood card for my turn. Bob is also a competitor and I've been cultivating a better relationship with Alice. Accepting might also annoy Alice, whom I just traded with. I will reject to ensure I can settle on my next turn.",
  "action": {
    "type": "trade_reject",
    "parameters": "{}"
  },
  "note_to_self": "Rejected Bob's 2 Wheat for 1 Wood. I have exactly 1W, 1B, 1S, 1Wh, 1O. Node 7 is open and I have a road to it. I will build my settlement there on my next turn to secure the Brick port.",
  "say_outloud": "I appreciate the offer, Bob, but I've got plans for that wood that involve a lot more than just some extra wheat. I'm going to have to pass."
}

{
  "internal_thinking": "Bob is offering 3 Wheat for 1 Wood. Currently, I have 1 Wood, 1 Brick, 1 Sheep, 1 Wheat, and 1 Ore. My immediate goal is to build a settlement at Node 7 (the Brick Port) on my next turn. I already have the necessary resources (1W, 1B, 1S, 1Wh) to do this. Accepting this trade would give me a massive surplus of Wheat (totaling 4) but would cost me the 1 Wood I need for my settlement. While 3:1 is a generous ratio, the Wood is more valuable to me right now for expansion. Bob is also a known schemer; giving him the Wood might help him build toward the port he's eyeing. I'll stick to my plan and keep the Wood for my settlement at Node 7.",
  "action": {
    "type": "trade_reject",
    "parameters": "{}"
  },
  "note_to_self": "Rejected Bob's 3 Wheat for 1 Wood. I still have 1W, 1B, 1S, 1Wh, 1O. Node 7 is open and I have a road connecting to it. I will build my settlement there on my next turn to secure the Brick port.",
  "say_outloud": "That's a tempting offer, Bob, really. But I've got my heart set on a project that needs that wood more than I need a stockpile of wheat. I'll pass."
}

{
  "internal_thinking": "It's the start of my turn. I currently have 1 Wood, 1 Brick, 1 Sheep, 1 Wheat, and 1 Ore. My goal for this turn is to build a settlement at Node 7, which is a Brick port (B2) and is connected to my road at Node 15. I have the exact resources required (W, B, S, Wh) to build a settlement. I don't have any development cards to play before the roll. I'll start by rolling the dice to see if I can gain any additional resources for future moves.",
  "action": {
    "type": "roll_dice",
    "parameters": "{}"
  },
  "note_to_self": "I have the resources (W, B, S, Wh) to build a settlement at Node 7 (Brick Port). I should execute this build immediately after the roll to secure the port and gain my 3rd VP.",
  "say_outloud": "Alright, let's see if the dice are as committed to my project as I am. Rolling!"
